    "### Example: Exponential Distribution\n",
    "\n",
    "Distrubtion of Death by Age in Australia in the Year 2012."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "11d8bb92",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "![Deaths in Australia in the year 2012](../../assets/deaths-in-australia-in-the-year-2012.png){height=480}"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "70ab7073",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "The figure above shows a **left-tailed exponential distribution**.\n",
    "\n",
    "The graph shows \"Deaths in Australia in the year 2012.\" This is an excellent example of a **Negatively Skewed (Left-Skewed)** distribution.\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Direction of Skew:** A distribution is named by the direction of its \"tail.\" Here, the tail extends toward the younger age brackets (the left). This indicates that while most people die at older ages, a smaller number of \"outliers\" die much younger, pulling the average down.\n",
    "* **The Relationship Between Mean, Median, and Mode:** In a perfectly symmetrical curve, these three would be the same. However, in a skewed distribution, they pull apart:\n",
    "    * **Mode (~87 years):** The \"most frequent\" age of death, represented by the highest peak.\n",
    "    * **Median (~81 years):** The middle value; 50% of people died before this age, and 50% after.\n",
    "    * **Mean (~77 years):** The arithmetic average. It is the most affected by the \"tail.\" Because some people die very young (ages 0–40), the mean is pulled significantly lower than the mode.\n",
    "* **Insight:** When data is skewed, the **Median** is often a better \"typical\" representation of the data than the Mean, because the Mean is overly sensitive to extreme outliers (infant mortality or early-life accidents)."
   ]
  },
